---
// THE DOWNLOAD BEAT: the scan (Adrian, 2026-08-19, corrected same day). The tool wall --
// every FS window receding to the horizon -- stands fullscreen BEHIND the room, hidden. The
// climbing band is a SLIT: only through it do you see the wall, the room carrying on above
// and below, and since the wall never moves the slit reads as an x-ray aperture scanning
// the scene. The download button rides the slit's middle, sitting on the wall it shows.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BracketButton from './BracketButton.astro';
import wall from '../assets/wall/FS_Tool_Wall.png';
---

<div class="wl" data-wall-overlay>
  {/* The wall: static, fullscreen, clipped to below the band's trailing edge. */}
  <div class="wl__wall">
    <Image src={wall} alt="" widths={[1600, 2560, 3162]} sizes="100vw" loading="lazy" />
  </div>

  {/* The scanner: an opaque band that climbs with the scroll, the download riding it. */}
  <div class="wl__band">
    <BracketButton kind="hard" href="/store/" label="Downloads" event="scan-download" />
    <BracketButton kind="plasma-outline" href="/services/" label="Services" event="scan-services" />
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* Fixed layer over the room (canvas z0) and the dead video (z1); under the copy layer
     (z3) and nav. Driven entirely by --scan (0..1) from the loop. */
  .wl {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    pointer-events: none;
    visibility: hidden;
    /* The band spans 16svh; its top runs from just past the bottom of the screen (108%)
       to fully above the top (-18%). One variable places everything. */
    --band-h: 16svh;
    --band-top: calc((1.08 - 1.26 * var(--scan, 0)) * 100%);
  }
  .wl[data-active] { visibility: visible; }
  .wl[data-active] :global(a),
  .wl[data-active] :global(button) { pointer-events: auto; }

  .wl__wall {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* Visible ONLY through the slit: clipped above the band's top edge and below its
       bottom edge. The image never moves; the aperture does. */
    clip-path: inset(var(--band-top) 0 calc(100% - var(--band-top) - var(--band-h)) 0);
  }
  .wl__wall :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .wl__band {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: var(--band-top);
    height: var(--band-h);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    /* No fill: the wall IS the band's interior. The edges are the aperture. */
    background: none;
    border-top: 1.5px solid var(--plasma);
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--plasma);
    box-shadow: 0 -5px 20px rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.18), 0 5px 20px rgba(124, 255, 178, 0.18);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  /* BURGER WIDTH: this beat needed the gate lifted and nothing else (Adrian, 2026-08-20).
     It was display:none here on the reasoning that "the cinematic never runs under 900px",
     true when written and false since the stage was opened up on 2026-08-20 -- so the phone
     got the canvas-side dissolve climbing the man, the wipe, with the aperture that explains
     it and both doors missing.

     No composition rule follows, deliberately. Everything in this beat is already centred or
     full-bleed: the band spans the screen, the doors are centred in it, and the wall is
     behind the slit rather than beside anything. The 16:9 wall cover-crops hard into a
     portrait frame, which here is a gain rather than a cost -- you get a few tool windows at
     a readable size instead of the full wall shrunk past recognition.

     Nothing hides this for the static fallback either: .wl is visibility:hidden until the
     loop sets [data-active], and on the fallback path the loop never runs. */
</style>
